## 🎯 Objective

Go from no project to a fully defined one: discover the right problem, scope a v1.0 that can actually be finished in nine remaining days, and produce a blueprint detailed enough that each remaining day is execution rather than re-planning.

No tech stack chosen today. No code written today. That was the brief's constraint and it was the right one.

---

## 🧭 The Project: Connector Gate

**Connector Gate** is a free, public, entirely client-side web tool that takes a security reviewer who has just been handed their first GenAI connector request — and has no AI-specific process for it — from intake to a defensible written verdict in one sitting.

It enforces a review that cannot silently skip anything: all six STRIDE categories, all ten OWASP LLM Top 10 (2025) categories, and a five-question insider-threat pass. Each must be assessed or explicitly marked "no applicable threat" *with a written reason*. It scores inherent and residual risk against fixed bands, refuses to credit any control that has not been evidenced, issues exactly one of three verdicts, and exports a CISO-ready memo in a fixed section order.

It runs on GitHub Pages. No account, no server, nothing leaving the browser.

### Why this project

The discovery interview settled three things in order, and each one narrowed the next.

**What is the capstone for?** Not a portfolio piece — **evidence of original contribution**. My EB-1A record's thinnest criterion is external adoption: I can show deep internal work, but "impact on my employer" is not "impact on the field," and USCIS treats those as different claims. A public, deployed tool that other practitioners actually use is the exact evidence that gap needs. That decision then constrained everything downstream: it had to be public, free, and usable without an account.

**Which project?** Four candidates. Connector Gate won because I already own the content — the methodology from 21 real connector reviews — so nine days goes entirely into building rather than learning the domain. The alternatives (an ISO 42001 readiness assessor, a framework crosswalk explorer, a prompt-injection harness) were either narrower, more content-heavy than engineering-heavy, or dependent on an endpoint I do not have.

**Where does it deploy?** GitHub Pages, which forces static-only. That constraint turned out to be an asset, not a limitation — see below.

