## 🧠 Key Learnings

**1. "What is this for?" had to be answered before "what is it?"**
I could have picked a project on interestingness and built something good. Asking what the capstone was *for* first — evidence of contribution, specifically — eliminated three of four candidates immediately and dictated the deployment target, the pricing (free), the account model (none), and the licence. Purpose is a scoping tool, not a preamble.

**2. The static-hosting constraint is the architecture.**
GitHub Pages means no server. That sounds limiting until you notice what it *guarantees*: no data at rest anywhere but the reviewer's own browser, nothing to breach, no account to create, no inference bill, and determinism by construction. For a tool whose users are handling other people's confidential architecture, "there is no server" is a stronger security claim than any control I could implement. The constraint stopped being something to work around and became the pitch.

**3. Naming one primary user is the real scope-creep defence.**
Not the out-of-scope list — that comes second. "A security engineer handed their first GenAI connector request, who has hours not weeks" answers feature questions on its own. Does this user need a multi-review register? No, they have one review. Does this user need team collaboration? No, they need to produce a document today. Four features fell away without a debate.

**4. Freezing the scoring spec on the design day is what makes testing possible on Day 58.**
The blueprint requires Day 52 to write ten hand-computed worked examples — input to expected output — and freeze them. Those examples *become* the test fixtures six days later. Tests written against a frozen spec catch real bugs; tests written against finished code just describe whatever the code already does.

**5. Writing the plan so a *different* assistant could continue it forced real specificity.**
Each remaining day begins a fresh conversation, so every day's section had to carry its own context. That constraint killed a lot of hand-waving. "Build the scoring engine" became reduction factors, the multiplication rule, the 10% floor, and the exact reason the floor exists. If a plan only works because the planner is in the room, it is notes, not a blueprint.

**6. I wrote the cut order before I needed it.**
CSV export → JSON import → OWASP sweep → insider pass. Decided today, calmly, with the whole project in view. On Day 57 at 11pm, tired and behind, is the worst possible moment to decide what matters least.

**7. Verify the load-bearing facts even when you think you know them.**
The app enforces all ten OWASP LLM categories *by name*. I checked the OWASP GenAI Security Project's own page rather than writing them from memory — the list changed between the 2023/24 and 2025 editions, and three of the current ten (System Prompt Leakage, Vector and Embedding Weaknesses, Unbounded Consumption) are not what I would have recalled. A tool whose whole value is rigour cannot ship a wrong category list.

